center-of-mass-velocity.conceptual_definition:
The formula 
$$ 
\mathbf{V}_{CM} = \dot{\mathbf{R}}_{CM} 
$$ 
states that the velocity of the center of mass (
$\mathbf{V}_{CM}$
) of a system of particles is precisely the time derivative of the position vector of the center of mass (
$\dot{\mathbf{R}}_{CM}$
). This relationship is fundamental in mechanics, particularly when analyzing the translational motion of extended objects or collections of particles as if they were a single point mass located at the center of mass.

center-of-mass-velocity.symmetry_origin:
This formula originates from the definition of velocity in classical mechanics and kinematics. Velocity is universally defined as the time rate of change of position. For any point in space, including the specially defined center of mass (
$\mathbf{R}_{CM}$
), its velocity (
$\mathbf{V}$
) is its time derivative (
$\dot{\mathbf{R}}$
). The concept of the center of mass itself is derived from the weighted average of the positions of all constituent particles in a system, and its translational motion can be shown to be independent of internal forces and determined solely by external forces, a consequence of Newton's laws, particularly the second law applied to the entire system (
$\mathbf{F}_{ext} = M_{total} \mathbf{a}_{CM} = M_{total} \dot{\mathbf{V}}_{CM} = M_{total} \ddot{\mathbf{R}}_{CM}$
).
